, evacuation); Does your organization provide a formal and accessible reporting mechanism for employees to report threats or concerning behavior. These gaps leave the organization in a difficult position if an incident occurs and post-incident review begins. Prioritizing the missing foundational controls will meaningfully reduce exposure and strengthen defensibility.','{\"q1\": \"no\", \"q2\": \"yes\", \"q3\": \"no\", \"q4\": \"no\", \"q5\": \"yes\", \"q6\": \"no\", \"q7\": \"no\", \"q8\": \"yes\", \"q9\": \"anon_informal\", \"q10\": \"ras_limited\", \"q11\": \"no\", \"q12\": \"yes\", \"q13\": \"no\", \"q14\": \"no\", \"q15\": \"yes\", \"q16\": \"no\"}','2026-05-06 14:22:17',36,'Moderate Risk'),(20,534,NULL,NULL,54,'High Exposure','High Exposure','orange','Elevated exposure with critical gaps requiring immediate attention.','AK — Alaska','Education (K-12 / Post-secondary)','[{\"id\": \"q2\", \"gap\": \"Do you have a documented Emergency Action Plan (EAP) that includes active threat response procedures (e.g., lockdown, lockout, evacuation, communication)?\", \"impact\": \"The absence of a documented EAP with active threat procedures may increase exposure significantly. OSHA 29 CFR 1910.38 requires a written EAP; the inclusion of active threat scenarios is a preparedness best practice. Post-incident reviews may cite this gap as evidence of inadequate preparedness.\", \"status\": \"Not in Place\", \"regulatoryBasis\": [\"OSHA 29 CFR 1910.38: Employers with more than 10 employees must maintain a written Emergency Action Plan covering evacuation, reporting, and employee accountability procedures.\", \"OSHA General Duty Clause (Section 5(a)(1)): Employers must address recognized hazards likely to cause death or serious physical harm. Workplace violence and active threat scenarios are recognized hazards in many industries.\"], \"preparednessBasis\": [\"CISA active threat preparedness principles recommend a documented active threat response plan as a foundational preparedness element.\", \"NFPA 3000 (Standard for an Active Shooter / Hostile Event Response Program) requires a documented hostile event response program including lockdown, lockout, and escape procedures.\"]}, {\"id\": \"q8\", \"gap\": \"Are incidents and near-misses consistently documented and tracked?\", \"impact\": \"Inconsistent documentation creates an evidentiary gap. In litigation, the inability to show a pattern of documented response suggests systemic negligence.\", \"status\": \"Not in Place\"}, {\"id\": \"q4\", \"gap\": \"Are employees trained to recognize pre-incident threat indicators and escalation behaviors?\", \"impact\": \"Failure to train employees to recognize warning signs is frequently cited in post-incident litigation as evidence that harm was foreseeable and preventable.\", \"status\": \"Not in Place\"}, {\"id\": \"q6\", \"gap\": \"Is training conducted at onboarding and refreshed on a defined and consistent schedule?\", \"impact\": \"One-time training without a defined refresh schedule is insufficient to demonstrate an ongoing commitment to employee safety and regulatory compliance.\", \"status\": \"Not in Place\"}, {\"id\": \"q11\", \"gap\": \"Are emergency or active threat drills conducted on a regular and defined basis?\", \"impact\": \"Organizations that fail to produce documented evidence of regular drills face significant exposure in post-incident reviews. Drills are evidence of operational commitment to safety.\", \"status\": \"Not in Place\"}]','{\"responseReadiness\": 22, \"trainingAwareness\": 45, \"planningDocumentation\": 56, \"reportingCommunication\": 84}','[\"Develop and formalize a Workplace Violence Prevention Policy and an Emergency Action Plan that includes active threat response procedures. These are the foundational documents required to establish any defensible posture — their absence is the single largest liability exposure in this assessment.\", \"Implement structured active threat training for all employees, covering threat recognition, escalation behaviors, and response protocols. Training must be documented, delivered at onboarding, and refreshed on a defined schedule to carry weight in post-incident review.\", \"Establish a formal internal reporting chain and incident tracking process. These systems create the evidentiary record that demonstrates the organization responded to warning signs before an incident escalated.\", \"Schedule and document active threat drills with defined roles and post-drill review. Drills without documentation provide no defensibility — the review and corrective action record is what matters in a post-incident examination.\", \"Implement a domestic violence or external personal threat protocol. This is a leading cause of workplace violence incidents and a frequently overlooked liability gap — organizations that fail to identify and manage this risk when known face significant exposure.\", \"Implement measures to identify and prevent insider threats, including employee risk indicator monitoring, accessible reporting pathways, and defined intervention protocols.\"]','This organization carries high liability exposure, with 8 control gaps that undermine its defensibility posture. Core planning and documentation requirements remain unmet, limiting the ability to demonstrate due diligence. Training gaps mean employees are not equipped to recognize or respond to active threat scenarios. Without a formal reporting process, threat indicators may go unaddressed until an incident occurs.  In the Education (K-12 / Post-secondary) sector, these gaps carry heightened scrutiny from regulators and plaintiff attorneys. Post-incident review would likely identify these gaps as evidence of systemic failure to protect.','Your organization operating in AK — Alaska in the Education (K-12 / Post-secondary) sector has high liability exposure with identifiable gaps that need to be addressed. Key areas of concern include: Do you have a documented Emergency Action Plan (EAP) that includes active threat response procedures (e.g., lockdown, lockout, evacuation, communication); Are incidents and near-misses consistently documented and tracked; Are employees trained to recognize pre-incident threat indicators and escalation behaviors. These gaps leave the organization in a difficult position if an incident occurs and post-incident review begins. Prioritizing the missing foundational controls will meaningfully reduce exposure and strengthen defensibility.','{\"q1\": \"yes\", \"q2\": \"no\", \"q3\": \"yes\", \"q4\": \"no\", \"q5\": \"yes\", \"q6\": \"no\", \"q7\": \"yes\", \"q8\": \"no\", \"q9\": \"anon_full\", \"q10\": \"ras_full\", \"q11\": \"no\", \"q12\": \"yes\", \"q13\": \"no\", \"q14\": \"no\", \"q15\": \"yes\", \"q16\": \"no\"}','2026-05-06 14:39:42',55,'Moderate Risk'),(35,214,NULL,NULL,44,'High Exposure','High Exposure','orange','Your readiness is heavily exposed. Major controls are missing or disconnected, which makes response harder to execute and harder to defend.','NY — New York','Retail','[{\"id\": \"q3\", \"gap\": \"Has a site-specific risk assessment been completed and documented for this store or facility, including public access areas, parking, entrances, checkout areas, back-of-house spaces, and late-night conditions where applicable?\", \"impact\": \"Without a documented site-specific risk assessment, the organization fails to meet the standard of due diligence — a core requirement in both regulatory defense and insurance claims.\", \"status\": \"Not in Place\", \"regulatoryBasis\": [\"OSHA General Duty Clause (Section 5(a)(1)): Employers must address recognized hazards likely to cause death or serious physical harm; where state law is silent, federal OSHA represents the baseline standard-of-care.\"]}, {\"id\": \"q10\", \"gap\": \"How does your organization immediately notify employees of an active threat and provide clear instructions?\", \"impact\": \"OSHA does not maintain a specific active-threat notification standard. However, the absence of a real-time alert system may increase exposure by delaying employee protective action and may weaken the organization\'s defensibility under the General Duty Clause, which requires employers to address recognized hazards. If an alarm system is used as part of the emergency action plan, it should align with OSHA 29 CFR 1910.38 and 1910.165 requirements. Delayed or absent notification during an active threat incident may be cited in post-incident litigation as evidence that the organization failed to take reasonable precautions. The absence of coordinated notification capability may undermine coordinated response and may be cited as evidence of inadequate preparedness.\", \"status\": \"Partial\", \"severity\": \"CRITICAL\", \"regulatoryBasis\": [\"OSHA General Duty Clause (Section 5(a)(1)): Employers must address recognized hazards. The absence of an employee notification capability may be cited as failure to address a recognized hazard.\", \"If the organization uses an employee alarm system as part of its emergency action plan, OSHA 29 CFR 1910.38 and 1910.165 govern related EAP and alarm requirements — these are not active-threat-specific mandates.\"], \"preparednessBasis\": [\"CISA active threat preparedness principles recommend real-time notification capability as a core element of coordinated active threat response.\", \"NFPA 3000 (Standard for an Active Shooter / Hostile Event Response Program) addresses coordinated notification and lockdown/lockout procedures as preparedness requirements.\"]}, {\"id\": \"q9\", \"gap\": \"What reporting option do employees have to report threats or concerning behavior?\", \"impact\": \"Without an anonymous reporting mechanism, employees who observe threatening or concerning behavior may not report it due to fear of retaliation or identification. This creates a systemic failure in early threat identification and escalation — the primary mechanism for preventing incidents before they occur. Post-incident reviews frequently find that warning signs existed but were not reported. In organizations without anonymous reporting channels, fear of retaliation is a recognized driver of non-reporting. The absence of an anonymous mechanism may be cited as evidence of inadequate preparedness and may weaken defensibility where California SB 553 or equivalent state requirements apply.\", \"status\": \"Incomplete\", \"severity\": \"HIGH\", \"regulatoryBasis\": [\"OSHA General Duty Clause (Section 5(a)(1)): Employers must address recognized hazards likely to cause death or serious physical harm; where state law is silent, federal OSHA represents the baseline standard-of-care.\"], \"preparednessBasis\": [\"CISA active threat preparedness principles recommend anonymous reporting channels as a key element of pre-incident threat identification and behavioral threat management.\", \"NFPA 3000 preparedness concepts support early threat identification through accessible, confidential reporting mechanisms.\"]}, {\"id\": \"q7\", \"gap\": \"Is there a clearly defined process for employees to report threatening customers, suspicious behavior, security concerns, or concerning interactions?\", \"impact\": \"Without a defined internal reporting chain, threat indicators have no pathway to supervisors or management. This is a system failure — not a gap. Post-incident reviews consistently find that employees observed warning signs but had no clear process for escalating them through the organization.\", \"status\": \"Not in Place\", \"regulatoryBasis\": [\"OSHA General Duty Clause (Section 5(a)(1)): Employers must address recognized hazards likely to cause death or serious physical harm; where state law is silent, federal OSHA represents the baseline standard-of-care.\"]}, {\"id\": \"q8\", \"gap\": \"Are threats, disruptive incidents, near-misses, and violence-related events consistently documented and tracked?\", \"impact\": \"Inconsistent documentation creates an evidentiary gap. In litigation, the inability to show a pattern of documented response suggests systemic negligence.\", \"status\": \"Not in Place\", \"regulatoryBasis\": [\"OSHA General Duty Clause (Section 5(a)(1)): Employers must address recognized hazards likely to cause death or serious physical harm; where state law is silent, federal OSHA represents the baseline standard-of-care.\"]}]','{\"responseReadiness\": 33, \"trainingAwareness\": 33, \"planningDocumentation\": 67, \"reportingCommunication\": 21}','[{\"action\": \"Conduct a Comprehensive, Site-Specific Workplace Violence Risk Assessment\", \"details\": \"Immediately undertake a detailed, documented risk assessment (addressing Q3) that specifically identifies hazards pertinent to a retail environment, including store layout, cash handling procedures, operating hours, public access points, parking lot security, common customer interactions, and isolated work areas. Use findings to tailor and update existing WPV policies and EAP.\", \"priority\": \"Critical\"}, {\"action\": \"Implement a Formal and Accessible Employee Reporting System\", \"details\": \"Establish a formal, confidential, and easily accessible pathway for employees to report suspicious behavior, security concerns, and incidents/near-misses (addressing Q7, Q9). This must include clear documentation and tracking procedures (addressing Q8) to ensure all reports are investigated and trends analyzed. Emphasize a \'see something, say something\' culture with clear follow-up.\", \"priority\": \"Critical\"}, {\"action\": \"Overhaul Workplace Violence Training Program\", \"details\": \"Develop and implement a robust WPV training program (addressing Q4, Q6). This must include: 1) training on recognizing pre-incident threat indicators and de-escalation techniques for customer-facing staff, 2) comprehensive active threat response (e.g., \'Run, Hide, Fight\'), and 3) a defined, consistent schedule for initial onboarding and mandatory annual refreshers for all employees. Document all training sessions.\", \"priority\": \"Critical\"}, {\"action\": \"Define and Communicate Emergency Response Roles and Responsibilities\", \"details\": \"Clearly define and document specific roles and responsibilities for all employees during emergency and active threat situations (addressing Q13). Communicate these roles effectively through training and visible postings. Conduct regular, documented drills (addressing Q11) that are reviewed for performance improvement and lessons learned (addressing Q12) to ensure clarity and effectiveness under stress.\", \"priority\": \"High\"}, {\"action\": \"Integrate External Personal Threat Management into WPV Program\", \"details\": \"Develop and implement a clear process for identifying and managing known domestic violence or external personal threat risks that may impact the workplace (addressing Q14). This should integrate with the existing \'individuals of concern\' process (Q15) and include protocols for employee support, security measures, and communication plans.\", \"priority\": \"High\"}]','This retail facility presents a critically underdeveloped workplace violence (WPV) readiness program, reflected in an overall score of 44. While foundational policies and some threat assessment mechanisms are present, significant operational gaps in site-specific risk identification, employee training consistency, formal reporting infrastructure, and response validation create a high-risk environment. The lack of consistent training on threat indicators and active threat response, combined with a nonexistent formal reporting pathway for employees, leaves the organization highly vulnerable to both preventable incidents and ineffective emergency response, particularly in a customer-facing retail setting. The current state poses a substantial risk to employee safety and regulatory compliance under the OSHA General Duty Clause.','This retail facility faces significant defensibility risks under the Federal OSHA General Duty Clause (29 U.S.C. § 654(a)(1)). The current state demonstrates a failure to provide a workplace \'free from recognized hazards\' that are causing or are likely to cause death or serious physical harm. Workplace violence, including robbery, customer aggression, and active threats, is a well-recognized hazard in the retail industry. The absence of a site-specific risk assessment (Q3), inconsistent and incomplete employee training (Q4, Q6), the severe lack of formal reporting mechanisms for threats and concerns (Q7, Q8, Q9), and untested, undefined emergency response roles (Q12, Q13) collectively indicate a clear failure to adequately identify, prevent, and respond to recognized WPV hazards. In the event of an incident, the organization would struggle to demonstrate it took reasonable and effective steps to protect its employees, especially given the clear guidance in OSHA\'s WPV Prevention Guidelines (best practice backstop).','{\"q1\": \"yes\", \"q2\": \"yes\", \"q3\": \"no\", \"q4\": \"no\", \"q5\": \"yes\", \"q6\": \"no\", \"q7\": \"no\", \"q8\": \"no\", \"q9\": \"anon_informal\", \"q10\": \"ras_basic\", \"q11\": \"yes\", \"q12\": \"no\", \"q13\": \"no\", \"q14\": \"no\", \"q15\": \"yes\", \"q16\": \"yes\"}','2026-05-24 15:59:52',44,'Major Readiness Gaps'),(36,214,NULL,NULL,75,'Moderate Readiness','Moderate Readiness','yellow-green','Most core elements exist, but one or more weak sections could fail under scrutiny.','CA — California','Healthcare','[{\"id\": \"q3\", \"gap\": \"Has a site-specific risk assessment been conducted and documented?\", \"impact\": \"Without a documented site-specific risk assessment, the organization fails to meet the standard of due diligence — a core requirement in both regulatory defense and insurance claims.\", \"status\": \"Not in Place\", \"regulatoryBasis\": [\"OSHA General Duty Clause (Section 5(a)(1)): Employers must address recognized hazards likely to cause death or serious physical harm; where state law is silent, federal OSHA represents the baseline standard-of-care.\"]}, {\"id\": \"q9\", \"gap\": \"Does your organization provide a formal and accessible reporting mechanism for employees to report threats or concerning behavior?\", \"impact\": \"Without an anonymous reporting mechanism, employees who observe threatening or concerning behavior may not report it due to fear of retaliation or identification. This creates a systemic failure in early threat identification and escalation — the primary mechanism for preventing incidents before they occur. Post-incident reviews frequently find that warning signs existed but were not reported. In organizations without anonymous reporting channels, fear of retaliation is a recognized driver of non-reporting. The absence of an anonymous mechanism may be cited as evidence of inadequate preparedness and may weaken defensibility where California SB 553 or equivalent state requirements apply.\", \"status\": \"Partial\", \"severity\": \"HIGH\", \"regulatoryBasis\": [\"OSHA General Duty Clause (Section 5(a)(1)): Employers must address recognized hazards likely to cause death or serious physical harm; where state law is silent, federal OSHA represents the baseline standard-of-care.\"], \"preparednessBasis\": [\"CISA active threat preparedness principles recommend anonymous reporting channels as a key element of pre-incident threat identification and behavioral threat management.\", \"NFPA 3000 preparedness concepts support early threat identification through accessible, confidential reporting mechanisms.\"]}, {\"id\": \"q7\", \"gap\": \"Is there a clearly defined internal process for employees to report suspicious behavior or security concerns?\", \"impact\": \"Without a defined internal reporting chain, threat indicators have no pathway to supervisors or management. This is a system failure — not a gap. Post-incident reviews consistently find that employees observed warning signs but had no clear process for escalating them through the organization.\", \"status\": \"Not in Place\", \"regulatoryBasis\": [\"OSHA General Duty Clause (Section 5(a)(1)): Employers must address recognized hazards likely to cause death or serious physical harm; where state law is silent, federal OSHA represents the baseline standard-of-care.\"]}, {\"id\": \"q4\", \"gap\": \"Are employees trained to recognize pre-incident threat indicators and escalation behaviors?\", \"impact\": \"Failure to train employees to recognize warning signs is frequently cited in post-incident litigation as evidence that harm was foreseeable and preventable.\", \"status\": \"Not in Place\", \"regulatoryBasis\": [\"OSHA General Duty Clause (Section 5(a)(1)): Employers must address recognized hazards likely to cause death or serious physical harm; where state law is silent, federal OSHA represents the baseline standard-of-care.\"]}, {\"id\": \"q12\", \"gap\": \"Are drills documented and reviewed for performance improvement?\", \"impact\": \"Undocumented drills provide no defensibility. Documentation of drill outcomes and corrective actions is required to demonstrate a continuous improvement posture.\", \"status\": \"Not in Place\", \"regulatoryBasis\": [\"OSHA General Duty Clause (Section 5(a)(1)): Employers must address recognized hazards likely to cause death or serious physical harm; where state law is silent, federal OSHA represents the baseline standard-of-care.\"]}]','{\"responseReadiness\": 67, \"trainingAwareness\": 67, \"planningDocumentation\": 67, \"reportingCommunication\": 65}','[\"Immediately conduct and document a comprehensive, site-specific workplace violence risk assessment. This assessment must identify potential hazards from patients, visitors, and employees, evaluate environmental factors, and inform the customization of the existing Workplace Violence Prevention Plan to meet Cal/OSHA 3342 and SB 553 requirements.\", \"Develop and implement mandatory training for all employees on recognizing pre-incident threat indicators and escalation behaviors. This training must include de-escalation techniques and be incorporated into initial onboarding and regular refresher schedules, as explicitly required by Cal/OSHA 3342.\", \"Establish and clearly communicate a defined internal process for employees to report suspicious behavior, security concerns, and workplace violence incidents. Ensure this process is accessible, promotes non-retaliation, and ideally offers an anonymous reporting option to encourage employee participation and early threat detection, aligning with Cal/OSHA 3342 and SB 553.\", \"Implement a system for documenting and reviewing all emergency and active threat drills. This review process should identify performance gaps, facilitate lessons learned, and drive continuous improvement of response protocols, ensuring optimal readiness and demonstrating due diligence.\", \"Review and update the existing Workplace Violence Prevention Policy to ensure it explicitly addresses all prescriptive elements of Cal/OSHA Title 8, Section 3342, including a clear structure for plan review, employee involvement, and alignment with the requirements of California SB 553, which is now effective.\"]','This healthcare facility demonstrates a foundational commitment to workplace violence prevention, particularly excelling in identifying and managing critical risk factors and implementing advanced active threat notification systems. However, the program exhibits significant vulnerabilities in core compliance areas as mandated by Cal/OSHA and the impending California SB 553. Key deficiencies include the absence of a site-specific risk assessment, critical gaps in employee training on threat recognition and de-escalation, and a lack of a clearly defined internal process for reporting suspicious behavior and security concerns. These gaps not only increase the facility\'s vulnerability to violent incidents but also pose substantial regulatory compliance risks, especially with SB 553 becoming effective July 1, 2024. Urgent action is required to address these foundational elements to ensure legal defensibility and enhance employee safety.','The facility faces significant legal and operational defensibility risks due to non-compliance with mandatory Cal/OSHA Title 8, Section 3342 and the newly effective California SB 553. Specifically, the absence of a site-specific risk assessment, critical gaps in mandated training (pre-incident indicators, de-escalation), and the lack of a defined employee reporting process for concerns represent direct violations that could lead to Cal/OSHA citations, penalties, and increased liability in the event of a workplace violence incident. Without these foundational elements, the facility\'s Workplace Violence Prevention Program is severely compromised, making it challenging to demonstrate due diligence in protecting employees and effectively mitigating legal challenges arising from a violent incident in the healthcare care setting.','{\"q1\": \"yes\", \"q2\": \"yes\", \"q3\": \"no\", \"q4\": \"no\", \"q5\": \"yes\", \"q6\": \"yes\", \"q7\": \"no\", \"q8\": \"yes\", \"q9\": \"anon_formal_only\", \"q10\": \"ras_full\", \"q11\": \"yes\", \"q12\": \"no\", \"q13\": \"yes\", \"q14\": \"yes\", \"q15\": \"yes\", \"q16\": \"yes\"}','2026-05-24 16:06:14',75,'Emerging Readiness'),(42,1,1,NULL,65,'Material Exposure','Material Exposure','yellow','Important gaps are creating material exposure. Your organization shows partial readiness, but key parts of the system are inconsistent, informal, or hard to prove.','ID — Idaho','Retail','[{\"id\": \"q4\", \"gap\": \"Are employees trained to recognize escalating customer behavior, suspicious conduct, stalking concerns, robbery indicators, and other pre-incident warning signs?\", \"impact\": \"Failure to train employees to recognize warning signs is frequently cited in post-incident litigation as evidence that harm was foreseeable and preventable.\", \"status\": \"Not in Place\", \"regulatoryBasis\": [\"OSHA General Duty Clause (Section 5(a)(1)): Employers must address recognized hazards likely to cause death or serious physical harm; where state law is silent, federal OSHA represents the baseline standard-of-care.\"]}, {\"id\": \"q5\", \"gap\": \"Are employees trained on active threat response for a public-facing retail environment, including customer communication, lockdown, evacuation, escape, or shelter decisions as applicable?\", \"impact\": \"Untrained employees in active threat scenarios may increase exposure. Post-incident litigation may examine whether the organization provided actionable response training. OSHA does not mandate a specific active shooter training standard, but the General Duty Clause requires employers to address recognized hazards, which includes providing employees with the means to respond.\", \"status\": \"Not in Place\", \"regulatoryBasis\": [\"OSHA General Duty Clause (Section 5(a)(1)): Employers must address recognized hazards likely to cause death or serious physical harm; where state law is silent, federal OSHA represents the baseline standard-of-care.\"], \"preparednessBasis\": [\"CISA active threat preparedness principles recommend Run-Hide-Fight or equivalent response training for all employees.\", \"NFPA 3000 requires training aligned with the organization\'s hostile event response program.\"]}, {\"id\": \"q6\", \"gap\": \"Is this training provided at onboarding and refreshed on a defined schedule for managers, sales floor staff, and support personnel?\", \"impact\": \"One-time training without a defined refresh schedule is insufficient to demonstrate an ongoing commitment to employee safety and regulatory compliance.\", \"status\": \"Not in Place\", \"regulatoryBasis\": [\"OSHA General Duty Clause (Section 5(a)(1)): Employers must address recognized hazards likely to cause death or serious physical harm; where state law is silent, federal OSHA represents the baseline standard-of-care.\"]}, {\"id\": \"q13\", \"gap\": \"Are roles and responsibilities clearly defined for store leadership, supervisors, floor staff, security, and after-hours personnel during an emergency?\", \"impact\": \"Undefined roles during an emergency create confusion, delayed response, and direct liability. Regulators and courts examine whether the organization had a clear chain of command.\", \"status\": \"Not in Place\", \"regulatoryBasis\": [\"OSHA General Duty Clause (Section 5(a)(1)): Employers must address recognized hazards likely to cause death or serious physical harm; where state law is silent, federal OSHA represents the baseline standard-of-care.\"]}, {\"id\": \"q14\", \"gap\": \"Are domestic violence or known external personal threat risks identified and managed when known, including concerns that may enter the workplace through customers, former partners, or targeted individuals?\", \"impact\": \"Domestic violence spillover is a leading cause of workplace homicide. Organizations that fail to identify and manage this risk when known face significant civil and regulatory exposure.\", \"status\": \"Not in Place\", \"regulatoryBasis\": [\"OSHA General Duty Clause (Section 5(a)(1)): Employers must address recognized hazards likely to cause death or serious physical harm; where state law is silent, federal OSHA represents the baseline standard-of-care.\"]}]','{\"responseReadiness\": 67, \"trainingAwareness\": 0, \"planningDocumentation\": 100, \"reportingCommunication\": 100}','[{\"action\": \"Implement comprehensive Workplace Violence Prevention training for all employees, focusing on recognizing pre-incident threat indicators, `de-escalation` techniques, and active threat response (e.g., lockdown, `lockout`, `escape`, `defend`). Prioritize `customer-facing` staff and ensure initial training is followed by regular, defined refreshers.\", \"priority\": \"Immediate\"}, {\"action\": \"Develop and formalize processes to identify, assess, and manage known domestic violence or external personal threats that could extend into the workplace. This includes clear communication pathways, protective measures, and support resources for affected employees. Simultaneously, establish protocols for identifying and preventing `insider threats` through behavioral indicators, robust reporting pathways, and intervention strategies.\", \"priority\": \"High\"}, {\"action\": \"Clearly define and document roles, responsibilities, and chains of command for all employees during emergency and active threat responses. Integrate these defined roles into existing Emergency Action Plans and incorporate them into all future drills to minimize `execution risk under stress`.\", \"priority\": \"High\"}, {\"action\": \"Establish a consistent, documented schedule for all workplace violence prevention training refreshers (e.g., annually) and emergency drills. Ensure drills are regularly reviewed, and lessons learned are integrated back into policies, procedures, and training.\", \"priority\": \"Medium\"}, {\"action\": \"Leverage existing strengths in documentation and robust reporting systems. Ensure that the site-specific risk assessment is regularly reviewed and updated to reflect new threats or changes in facility operations, especially concerning `public access` areas like the `store` and `parking lot`.\", \"priority\": \"Medium\"}]','This retail facility demonstrates strong foundational planning and robust reporting mechanisms for workplace violence prevention. However, a critical vulnerability lies in the complete absence of employee training across all aspects of workplace violence awareness and response. This is further compounded by significant gaps in managing known external (domestic/personal) and internal (insider) threats, and a lack of defined roles for emergency response, which introduces substantial execution risk during an actual incident. The overall score of 65 reflects these critical deficiencies, and the \'critical_risk_factors\' hard-stop flag signals immediate and severe risks, particularly given the public-facing nature of retail operations.','The facility faces significant legal defensibility risk, primarily under Federal OSHA\'s General Duty Clause (29 U.S.C. § 654(a)(1)). The complete absence of employee training (Q4, Q5, Q6) on recognized workplace violence hazards, including threat recognition and active threat response, constitutes a direct failure to provide a workplace free from recognized hazards. This is further exacerbated by the failure to identify and manage known external personal threats (Q14) and `insider threats` (Q16), which are significant, recognized hazards in a `public access` retail setting. While robust documentation and reporting exist, the lack of trained personnel and unmanaged critical risk factors create a substantial gap in practical preparedness. In the event of an incident, the organization would be highly vulnerable to findings of willful or serious violations by Federal OSHA, especially given the availability of industry-specific best practices like OSHA Guidelines for Preventing Workplace Violence for Retail Workers and ANSI/ASIS WVPI.1-2021, which strongly emphasize training and comprehensive threat management.','{\"q1\": \"yes\", \"q2\": \"yes\", \"q3\": \"yes\", \"q4\": \"no\", \"q5\": \"no\", \"q6\": \"no\", \"q7\": \"yes\", \"q8\": \"yes\", \"q9\": \"anon_full\", \"q10\": \"ras_full\", \"q11\": \"yes\", \"q12\": \"yes\", \"q13\": \"no\", \"q14\": \"no\", \"q15\": \"yes\", \"q16\": \"no\"}','2026-06-10 17:24:26',60,'Significant Readiness Gaps'),(43,1,1,NULL,48,'High Exposure','High Exposure','orange','Your readiness is heavily exposed. Major controls are missing or disconnected, which makes response harder to execute and harder to defend.','HI — Hawaii','Education (K-12 / Post-secondary)','[{\"id\": \"q3\", \"gap\": \"Has a site-specific risk assessment been conducted and documented?\", \"impact\": \"Without a documented site-specific risk assessment, the organization fails to meet the standard of due diligence — a core requirement in both regulatory defense and insurance claims.\", \"status\": \"Not in Place\", \"regulatoryBasis\": [\"OSHA General Duty Clause (Section 5(a)(1)): Employers must address recognized hazards likely to cause death or serious physical harm; where state law is silent, federal OSHA represents the baseline standard-of-care.\"]}, {\"id\": \"q7\", \"gap\": \"Is there a clearly defined internal process for employees to report suspicious behavior or security concerns?\", \"impact\": \"Without a defined internal reporting chain, threat indicators have no pathway to supervisors or management. This is a system failure — not a gap. Post-incident reviews consistently find that employees observed warning signs but had no clear process for escalating them through the organization.\", \"status\": \"Not in Place\", \"regulatoryBasis\": [\"OSHA General Duty Clause (Section 5(a)(1)): Employers must address recognized hazards likely to cause death or serious physical harm; where state law is silent, federal OSHA represents the baseline standard-of-care.\"]}, {\"id\": \"q4\", \"gap\": \"Are employees trained to recognize pre-incident threat indicators and escalation behaviors?\", \"impact\": \"Failure to train employees to recognize warning signs is frequently cited in post-incident litigation as evidence that harm was foreseeable and preventable.\", \"status\": \"Not in Place\", \"regulatoryBasis\": [\"OSHA General Duty Clause (Section 5(a)(1)): Employers must address recognized hazards likely to cause death or serious physical harm; where state law is silent, federal OSHA represents the baseline standard-of-care.\"]}, {\"id\": \"q6\", \"gap\": \"Is training conducted at onboarding and refreshed on a defined and consistent schedule?\", \"impact\": \"One-time training without a defined refresh schedule is insufficient to demonstrate an ongoing commitment to employee safety and regulatory compliance.\", \"status\": \"Not in Place\", \"regulatoryBasis\": [\"OSHA General Duty Clause (Section 5(a)(1)): Employers must address recognized hazards likely to cause death or serious physical harm; where state law is silent, federal OSHA represents the baseline standard-of-care.\"]}, {\"id\": \"q1\", \"gap\": \"Do you have a documented Workplace Violence Prevention Policy that defines prohibited behaviors, reporting expectations, and response protocols?\", \"impact\": \"Without a documented policy, the organization fails to meet the standard of due diligence to prevent workplace violence — a foundational element in civil liability defense.\", \"status\": \"Not in Place\", \"regulatoryBasis\": [\"OSHA General Duty Clause (Section 5(a)(1)): Employers must address recognized hazards likely to cause death or serious physical harm; where state law is silent, federal OSHA represents the baseline standard-of-care.\"]}]','{\"responseReadiness\": 67, \"trainingAwareness\": 33, \"planningDocumentation\": 33, \"reportingCommunication\": 75}','[{\"action\": \"Develop and formally adopt a comprehensive Workplace Violence Prevention Policy. This policy must clearly define prohibited behaviors, reporting expectations, and response protocols, serving as the foundational document for all WPV efforts. (Addresses q1)\", \"priority\": \"Immediate\"}, {\"action\": \"Conduct a thorough, site-specific workplace violence risk assessment. This assessment must identify potential threats, vulnerabilities, and existing controls, informing all prevention and response strategies. (Addresses q3)\", \"priority\": \"Immediate\"}, {\"action\": \"Establish and implement a comprehensive threat assessment and behavioral intervention process. This process must explicitly cover identification and management of all relevant risk factors, including domestic violence spillover, external personal threats, and potential insider threats. This should be a multidisciplinary team effort. (Addresses q14, q15, q16)\", \"priority\": \"High\"}, {\"action\": \"Develop and implement a structured, ongoing training program for all employees. This program must include training on recognizing pre-incident threat indicators, de-escalation techniques, and active threat response. Training should be mandatory at onboarding and refreshed on a defined, consistent schedule. (Addresses q4, q5, q6)\", \"priority\": \"High\"}, {\"action\": \"Clearly define and document roles and responsibilities for all personnel involved in emergency response. Integrate these roles into the EAP and reinforce them through drills to ensure clarity and accountability during an incident. (Addresses q13)\", \"priority\": \"Medium\"}, {\"action\": \"Refine and clearly communicate internal processes for employees to report suspicious behavior, security concerns, and potential threats, ensuring clear pathways for both formal and informal reporting. (Addresses q7, q9)\", \"priority\": \"Medium\"}]','The organization exhibits significant deficiencies in fundamental workplace violence prevention planning, documentation, and comprehensive threat management. Despite some strengths in immediate communication and active threat response execution, the absence of a documented policy, site-specific risk assessment, and consistent preventative training creates substantial legal and operational vulnerabilities. The presence of hard-stop flags for \'Planning & Documentation\' and \'Critical Risk Factors\' underscores immediate, high-priority areas of concern, indicating a reactive rather than proactive safety posture. The current state suggests a lack of systematic preparation required to meet the \'General Duty Clause\' under HIOSH, emphasizing unaddressed recognized hazards and a limited ability to demonstrate due diligence.','The organization faces substantial legal defensibility risks due to its failure to meet the HIOSH General Duty Clause. The absence of a documented Workplace Violence Prevention Policy and a site-specific risk assessment directly demonstrates a failure to identify and mitigate \'recognized hazards.\' Furthermore, the lack of comprehensive preventative training, inconsistent training cadences, and critically, the failure to identify and manage specific high-risk factors like external/domestic violence and insider threats, indicate a reactive and incomplete approach. In the event of a workplace violence incident, the organization would struggle to demonstrate that it provided a workplace \'free from recognized hazards that are causing or are likely to cause death or serious physical harm to employees.\' The reliance on specific industry guidance (U.S. Dept. of Education / DHS) as best practice standards further highlights the deficiency, as many core elements of comprehensive school safety plans are currently unmet.','{\"q1\": \"no\", \"q2\": \"yes\", \"q3\": \"no\", \"q4\": \"no\", \"q5\": \"yes\", \"q6\": \"no\", \"q7\": \"no\", \"q8\": \"yes\", \"q9\": \"anon_full\", \"q10\": \"ras_full\", \"q11\": \"yes\", \"q12\": \"yes\", \"q13\": \"no\", \"q14\": \"no\", \"q15\": \"yes\", \"q16\": \"no\"}','2026-06-10 21:14:17',48,'Major Readiness Gaps'),(44,1,1,NULL,50,'Material Exposure','Material Exposure','yellow','Foundational elements exist but are not yet consistent enough to ensure reliable execution. Strengthening these areas will improve documentation, response capability, and organizational defensibility.','IL — Illinois','Education (K-12 / Post-secondary)','[{\"id\": \"q3\", \"gap\": \"Has a site-specific risk assessment been conducted and documented?\", \"impact\": \"Without a documented site-specific risk assessment, the organization fails to meet the standard of due diligence — a core requirement in both regulatory defense and insurance claims.\", \"status\": \"Not in Place\", \"regulatoryBasis\": [\"OSHA General Duty Clause (Section 5(a)(1)): Employers must address recognized hazards likely to cause death or serious physical harm; where state law is silent, federal OSHA represents the baseline standard-of-care.\"]}, {\"id\": \"q7\", \"gap\": \"Is there a clearly defined internal process for employees to report suspicious behavior or security concerns?\", \"impact\": \"Without a defined internal reporting chain, threat indicators have no pathway to supervisors or management. This is a system failure — not a gap. Post-incident reviews consistently find that employees observed warning signs but had no clear process for escalating them through the organization.\", \"status\": \"Not in Place\", \"regulatoryBasis\": [\"OSHA General Duty Clause (Section 5(a)(1)): Employers must address recognized hazards likely to cause death or serious physical harm; where state law is silent, federal OSHA represents the baseline standard-of-care.\"]}, {\"id\": \"q8\", \"gap\": \"Are incidents and near-misses consistently documented and tracked?\", \"impact\": \"Inconsistent documentation creates an evidentiary gap. In litigation, the inability to show a pattern of documented response suggests systemic negligence.\", \"status\": \"Not in Place\", \"regulatoryBasis\": [\"OSHA General Duty Clause (Section 5(a)(1)): Employers must address recognized hazards likely to cause death or serious physical harm; where state law is silent, federal OSHA represents the baseline standard-of-care.\"]}, {\"id\": \"q1\", \"gap\": \"Do you have a documented Workplace Violence Prevention Policy that defines prohibited behaviors, reporting expectations, and response protocols?\", \"impact\": \"Without a documented policy, the organization fails to meet the standard of due diligence to prevent workplace violence — a foundational element in civil liability defense.\", \"status\": \"Not in Place\", \"regulatoryBasis\": [\"OSHA General Duty Clause (Section 5(a)(1)): Employers must address recognized hazards likely to cause death or serious physical harm; where state law is silent, federal OSHA represents the baseline standard-of-care.\"]}, {\"id\": \"q11\", \"gap\": \"Are emergency or active threat drills conducted on a regular and defined basis?\", \"impact\": \"Organizations that fail to produce documented evidence of regular drills face significant exposure in post-incident reviews. Drills are evidence of operational commitment to safety.\", \"status\": \"Not in Place\", \"regulatoryBasis\": [\"OSHA General Duty Clause (Section 5(a)(1)): Employers must address recognized hazards likely to cause death or serious physical harm; where state law is silent, federal OSHA represents the baseline standard-of-care.\"]}]','{\"responseReadiness\": 33, \"trainingAwareness\": 100, \"planningDocumentation\": 33, \"reportingCommunication\": 50}','[\"Immediate: Develop and Formally Implement a Comprehensive Workplace Violence Prevention Plan (WVPP)\", \"Immediate: Conduct Site-Specific Risk Assessments Across All Facilities\", \"High: Establish and Execute a Regular Emergency Drill Schedule\", \"High: Formalize and Standardize Internal Reporting and Incident Documentation\", \"Moderate: Clearly Define and Communicate Emergency Response Roles and Responsibilities\", \"Moderate: Enhance Comprehensive Threat Management Processes\"]','Our assessment of your organization\'s workplace violence prevention posture indicates a foundational commitment to employee training and formal reporting mechanisms, reflected in strong \'training_awareness\' and effective \'reporting_communication\' systems. However, a significant gap exists in the overarching planning, documentation, and practical validation of these efforts, leading to an overall score of 50. Key deficiencies in \'planning_documentation\' and \'response_readiness\' create substantial organizational vulnerabilities. While employees are trained, the absence of a comprehensive written plan, site-specific risk assessments, and regular emergency drills prevents the system from operating cohesively. This exposes the organization to elevated risks in terms of legal defensibility, operational resilience, and the sustained psychological safety of its community. A proactive and integrated approach to these areas is critical for ensuring a safer environment and mitigating future liabilities.','The current state of your organization\'s workplace violence prevention program presents significant legal defensibility risks. The absence of a documented Workplace Violence Prevention Policy and site-specific risk assessments (Q1, Q3) directly violates the explicit requirements of the Illinois School Code (for K-12) and the Illinois Higher Education Violence Prevention Act (for public post-secondary institutions), which mandate comprehensive safety plans and risk mitigation. Furthermore, the lack of regular emergency drills (Q11) and incident documentation (Q8) undermines the ability to demonstrate \'due diligence\' and \'reasonable care\' as required by the OSHA General Duty Clause. In the event of an incident, the organization would struggle to produce verifiable evidence of proactive prevention, systematic risk management, or validated response protocols. This exposure could lead to findings of negligence, substantial regulatory penalties from state or federal OSHA, and adverse outcomes in civil litigation, significantly impacting financial stability and institutional reputation.','{\"q1\": \"no\", \"q2\": \"yes\", \"q3\": \"no\", \"q4\": \"yes\", \"q5\": \"yes\", \"q6\": \"yes\", \"q7\": \"no\", \"q8\": \"no\", \"q9\": \"anon_full\", \"q10\": \"ras_full\", \"q11\": \"no\", \"q12\": \"yes\", \"q13\": \"no\", \"q14\": \"no\", \"q15\": \"yes\", \"q16\": \"no\"}','2026-07-25 07:12:50',50,'Readiness Improvements Needed'),(45,1,1,NULL,74,'Moderate Readiness','Moderate Readiness','yellow-green','Most core elements are in place. Focused attention on the remaining areas will strengthen overall system reliability and organizational confidence.','DE — Delaware','Education (K-12 / Post-secondary)','[{\"id\": \"q10\", \"gap\": \"Does your organization have a method to immediately notify employees of an active threat and provide clear instructions (e.g., lockdown, evacuation)?\", \"impact\": \"OSHA does not maintain a specific active-threat notification standard. However, the absence of a real-time alert system may increase exposure by delaying employee protective action and may weaken the organization\'s defensibility under the General Duty Clause, which requires employers to address recognized hazards. If an alarm system is used as part of the emergency action plan, it should align with OSHA 29 CFR 1910.38 and 1910.165 requirements. Delayed or absent notification during an active threat incident may be cited in post-incident litigation as evidence that the organization failed to take reasonable precautions. The absence of coordinated notification capability may undermine coordinated response and may be cited as evidence of inadequate preparedness.\", \"status\": \"Partial\", \"severity\": \"CRITICAL\", \"regulatoryBasis\": [\"OSHA General Duty Clause (Section 5(a)(1)): Employers must address recognized hazards. The absence of an employee notification capability may be cited as failure to address a recognized hazard.\", \"If the organization uses an employee alarm system as part of its emergency action plan, OSHA 29 CFR 1910.38 and 1910.165 govern related EAP and alarm requirements — these are not active-threat-specific mandates.\"], \"preparednessBasis\": [\"CISA active threat preparedness principles recommend real-time notification capability as a core element of coordinated active threat response.\", \"NFPA 3000 (Standard for an Active Shooter / Hostile Event Response Program) addresses coordinated notification and lockdown/lockout procedures as preparedness requirements.\"]}, {\"id\": \"q9\", \"gap\": \"Does your organization provide a formal and accessible reporting mechanism for employees to report threats or concerning behavior?\", \"impact\": \"Without an anonymous reporting mechanism, employees who observe threatening or concerning behavior may not report it due to fear of retaliation or identification. This creates a systemic failure in early threat identification and escalation — the primary mechanism for preventing incidents before they occur. Post-incident reviews frequently find that warning signs existed but were not reported. In organizations without anonymous reporting channels, fear of retaliation is a recognized driver of non-reporting. The absence of an anonymous mechanism may be cited as evidence of inadequate preparedness and may weaken defensibility where California SB 553 or equivalent state requirements apply.\", \"status\": \"Incomplete\", \"severity\": \"HIGH\", \"regulatoryBasis\": [\"OSHA General Duty Clause (Section 5(a)(1)): Employers must address recognized hazards likely to cause death or serious physical harm; where state law is silent, federal OSHA represents the baseline standard-of-care.\"], \"preparednessBasis\": [\"CISA active threat preparedness principles recommend anonymous reporting channels as a key element of pre-incident threat identification and behavioral threat management.\", \"NFPA 3000 preparedness concepts support early threat identification through accessible, confidential reporting mechanisms.\"]}, {\"id\": \"q11\", \"gap\": \"Are emergency or active threat drills conducted on a regular and defined basis?\", \"impact\": \"Organizations that fail to produce documented evidence of regular drills face significant exposure in post-incident reviews. Drills are evidence of operational commitment to safety.\", \"status\": \"Not in Place\", \"regulatoryBasis\": [\"OSHA General Duty Clause (Section 5(a)(1)): Employers must address recognized hazards likely to cause death or serious physical harm; where state law is silent, federal OSHA represents the baseline standard-of-care.\"]}, {\"id\": \"q13\", \"gap\": \"Are roles and responsibilities clearly defined during an emergency response?\", \"impact\": \"Undefined roles during an emergency create confusion, delayed response, and direct liability. Regulators and courts examine whether the organization had a clear chain of command.\", \"status\": \"Not in Place\", \"regulatoryBasis\": [\"OSHA General Duty Clause (Section 5(a)(1)): Employers must address recognized hazards likely to cause death or serious physical harm; where state law is silent, federal OSHA represents the baseline standard-of-care.\"]}, {\"id\": \"q16\", \"gap\": \"Does your organization have measures in place to identify and prevent insider threats or acts of internal violence (e.g., employee risk indicators, reporting pathways, intervention protocols)?\", \"impact\": \"Insider threats and acts of internal violence are among the most preventable forms of workplace violence. Organizations without structured risk indicator monitoring, reporting pathways, and intervention protocols may face significant exposure when internal warning signs were present but unaddressed.\", \"status\": \"Not in Place\", \"regulatoryBasis\": [\"OSHA General Duty Clause (Section 5(a)(1)): Employers must address recognized hazards. The absence of a reporting mechanism may be cited as evidence of inadequate hazard identification infrastructure.\", \"State-level equivalents to California SB 553 are emerging in multiple jurisdictions — organizations should verify applicable state requirements.\"], \"preparednessBasis\": [\"CISA active threat preparedness principles recommend anonymous reporting channels as a key element of pre-incident threat identification and behavioral threat management.\", \"NFPA 3000 preparedness concepts support early threat identification through accessible, confidential reporting mechanisms.\"]}]','{\"responseReadiness\": 33, \"trainingAwareness\": 100, \"planningDocumentation\": 100, \"reportingCommunication\": 71}','[\"Critical: Implement a Formal and Accessible Threat Reporting System\", \"Critical: Establish and Execute a Regular Emergency Drill Schedule\", \"Critical: Define and Communicate Clear Roles and Responsibilities for Emergency Response\", \"High: Develop and Implement Insider Threat Prevention Measures\", \"High: Enhance Mass Notification Systems\"]','The organization demonstrates a strong foundation in documented Workplace Violence Prevention (WVP) policies, Emergency Action Plans (EAP), and initial employee training across critical areas, including active threat response and threat indicator recognition. This proactive stance on foundational planning and awareness is commendable and provides a crucial framework for safety. However, a comprehensive assessment reveals significant vulnerabilities in validating these plans through practice, establishing formal reporting mechanisms, and addressing specific critical risk factors. The absence of regular emergency drills, undefined response roles, and a lack of a formal reporting system for threats represent key areas of elevated risk, potentially hindering effective incident response and compromising organizational resilience. Addressing these gaps is paramount to ensuring employee safety, operational readiness, and legal defensibility.','While the organization has established fundamental Workplace Violence Prevention policies, Emergency Action Plans, and initial employee training, its current state of preparedness presents a significant legal defensibility risk. The absence of regular emergency drills to validate response protocols, the lack of clearly defined roles and responsibilities during a crisis, and the reliance on informal threat reporting mechanisms indicate a substantial gap in the practical application of due diligence and reasonable care. In the event of a workplace violence incident, these deficiencies could expose the organization to findings of negligence under the OSHA General Duty Clause and state-specific safety mandates for schools. The failure to rigorously test plans and establish formal reporting pathways suggests that while the intent to protect employees exists on paper, the demonstrable implementation of a fully operational and validated prevention and response system is insufficient, thereby increasing legal liability and potential regulatory scrutiny.','{\"q1\": \"yes\", \"q2\": \"yes\", \"q3\": \"yes\", \"q4\": \"yes\", \"q5\": \"yes\", \"q6\": \"yes\", \"q7\": \"yes\", \"q8\": \"yes\", \"q9\": \"anon_informal\", \"q10\": \"ras_basic\", \"q11\": \"no\", \"q12\": \"yes\", \"q13\": \"no\", \"q14\": \"yes\", \"q15\": \"yes\", \"q16\": \"no\"}','2026-07-27 10:54:31',74,'Emerging Readiness');
/*!40000 ALTER TABLE `liability_scans` ENABLE KEYS */;
